# Statement and Assumption

> A statement makes sense only if its author silently believes a few
> things that the statement never spells out. This lesson defines those
> hidden beliefs as assumptions, sorts them into types, and drills the
> exam verdict — definitely implicit or definitely not — with eight
> fully solved questions.

[important]
An assumption is neither what the statement proves nor what the reader
should do. It is the *premise the author must already believe* for the
statement to make sense at all. Sit in the author's chair, not the
reader's, before judging any option.
[/important]

## 1. What an Assumption Is

Every notice, advertisement, or announcement is written for a purpose,
and that purpose rests on beliefs the writer leaves unsaid. Those
unsaid, necessary beliefs are the assumptions.

[definition]
**Assumption**: something taken for granted — an unstated premise that
must be true for the given statement to be sensible, relevant, or
justified. It is never written directly in the statement; if the
statement itself says it, it is explicit and not an assumption.
[/definition]

A typical exam question looks like this:

```text
Statement: "Buy Shakti soap — it removes stains in one wash."
Assumption I:   A single wash is enough for a buyer to judge a soap.
Assumption II:  People read advertisements before buying soap.
```

You must decide for each assumption whether it is *implicit* — genuinely
and definitely hidden behind the statement — or not. Here, Assumption I
is implicit (the claim "one wash" is meaningless unless one wash can be
noticed), while Assumption II is not definitely required: the ad targets
buyers, but it does not need the belief that they read ads first.

## 2. Implicit vs Explicit

| Term | Meaning | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Explicit | written in the statement itself | "the library opens at 8 a.m." |
| Implicit | hidden, but required for the statement to work | "the library is closed before 8 a.m." |

Only implicit material counts as an assumption. This is why an option
that merely *repeats* the statement is a trap: restating the text is not
assuming something behind it.

[warning]
An assumption must be *necessary*, not merely *possible*. If the
statement still makes perfect sense without a belief, that belief is not
an assumption — it is only one of many optional thoughts. Ask: "Would
the author still have written this sentence if he did not believe X?"
If yes, X is not implicit.
[/warning]

## 3. Types of Assumptions

@diagram tree id="assumption-types-tree" layout="tree" aria="Four common types of implicit assumptions"
  Assumptions -> Necessity
  Assumptions -> Motivation
  Assumptions -> Message
  Assumptions -> Comparison
  Necessity -> "the announced facility or action is needed"
  Motivation -> "people desire the promised benefit"
  Message -> "the audience will notice and understand the text"
  Comparison -> "the offered option is good, sufficient, or better"
@/diagram

Most exam assumptions fall into one of these four bins. Recognising the
bin gives you the option's likely wording before you even read it: an
advertisement usually assumes desire, a notice assumes need, an appeal
assumes cooperation, and a course announcement assumes sufficiency.

The statement texts that carry these questions are also predictable. A
count over a typical 20-question practice set looks like this:

@chart bar title="Assumption questions by statement source in a 20-question practice set"
labels:
Notices and orders
Advertisements
Warnings
Appeals
Policy announcements

data:
6
5
4
3
2
@/chart

Notices and advertisements together supply more than half the block, so
the solved examples in the next two sections concentrate on exactly
those two sources.

## 4. The Judgement Test

Run every option through the same three steps:

1. **Find the hidden link** the author needs: who is expected to do
   what, and why would they do it?
2. **Match the option to the link** at the same strength — no wider,
   no narrower.
3. **Apply the necessity check**: remove the belief from the author's
   mind; if the statement still stands untouched, the option is not
   implicit.

@diagram flowchart id="assumption-judgement-flow" layout="hierarchical" aria="Decision flow for judging an assumption"
node start label="Read the statement" shape="pill"
node link label="Find the hidden link the author needs" shape="round-rect"
node compare label="Does the option match the link exactly?" shape="diamond"
node yes label="Implicit" shape="round-rect"
node no label="Not implicit" shape="round-rect"

start -> link
link -> compare
compare -> yes label="yes"
compare -> no label="no"
@/diagram

[tip]
Watch for *negative* assumptions — beliefs phrased as "not", "unless",
or "otherwise". A warning sign "Do not enter — the bridge is weak"
assumes that people *might* otherwise enter. The presence of the danger
implies the possibility of the act; that possibility is the assumption.
[/tip]

## 5. Solved Examples: Notices and Advertisements

**Example 1.**
Statement: "Use Brightwhite toothpaste for whiter teeth in just seven
days." Assumption: "People desire whiter teeth." → *Implicit.* An
advertisement argues from a benefit, and the benefit only motivates
buyers who already desire it; without this belief the ad has no pull.

**Example 2.**
Statement: "All employees must attend the fire-safety drill on Friday."
Assumption: "Compulsion is an acceptable way to secure attendance." →
*Implicit.* The word "must" orders compliance, and an order is issued
only by an authority that accepts compulsion as legitimate for the end
in view.

**Example 3.**
Statement: "Buy one, get one free — offer valid this week only."
Assumption: "Customers are more likely to buy when an offer has a
deadline." → *Implicit.* "This week only" manufactures urgency; the
phrase would be pointless unless the seller believed urgency increases
purchases.

**Example 4.**
Statement: "Shop at MegaMart — prices lower than any other store in
town." Assumption: "No other store in town matches MegaMart's prices."
→ *Not implicit.* This option *repeats* the explicit comparison written
in the statement; it is stated, so it is not an assumption.

## 6. Solved Examples: Warnings, Appeals, and Policies

**Example 5.**
Statement: "Do not go near the river; the water level is rising."
Assumption: "People might otherwise go near the river." → *Implicit.*
A warning is wasted on an act nobody could commit; the very act of
warning assumes the possibility of the act being warned against.

**Example 6.**
Statement: "The government has increased the scholarship amount for
meritorious students." Assumption: "Higher scholarships encourage
meritorious students." → *Implicit.* A policy change claims a benefit;
the authority must believe the change produces that benefit, otherwise
the decision has no justification.

**Example 7.**
Statement: "Save water — every drop counts. — Municipal Corporation."
Assumption: "Citizens can reduce their water usage." → *Implicit.* An
appeal to citizens assumes they are capable of responding to it; an
appeal addressed to the powerless would be meaningless.

**Example 8.**
Statement: "Enrol in the crash course to score 90+ in the board exam."
Assumption: "The crash course is sufficient help for reaching that
score." → *Implicit.* The invitation promises a target through the
course; the writer must believe the course can carry a student to it.

[example]
Verification method for every solved item above: negate the assumption
and reread the statement. "People do NOT desire whiter teeth" destroys
the advertisement; "compulsion is NOT acceptable" destroys the order.
A belief whose negation wrecks the statement is exactly what makes it
implicit. If negation leaves the statement standing, the option is not
implicit.
[/example]

## 7. Traps: Too Broad, Too Narrow, Wrong Direction

| Trap | Signature | Example of the wrong option |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Too broad | pushes beyond everyone/everywhere | "all citizens obey all notices" |
| Too narrow | shrinks a general claim to one case | "only students need the library" |
| Restatement | copies words from the statement | the explicit claim re-labelled "assumption" |
| Wrong direction | gives the reader's belief, not the author's | "buyers should check prices elsewhere" |
| Out of scope | imports an unrelated fact | "soap is cheaper than shampoo" |

[warning]
The two most common errors are treating the *conclusion* as an
assumption and demanding *certainty about the world*. An assumption is
judged relative to the statement, not relative to reality: it can sound
false or exaggerated and still be implicit, because the author clearly
believed it.
[/warning]

[info]
Both-implicit questions: when the question asks "which assumption(s) is/are implicit?", test each
independently. Answer patterns: both, only I, only II, or neither — and
"both" is a perfectly normal answer, so never force exactly one to be
correct.
[/info]

## 8. Quick Revision

| Concept | One-line rule |
| --- | --- |
| Assumption | unstated premise the author must believe |
| Explicit vs implicit | written text is explicit; only the hidden premise is an assumption |
| Necessity check | if negating the belief wrecks the statement, it is implicit |
| Warnings | assume the warned act is possible |
| Advertisements | assume the audience desires the benefit |
| Orders and notices | assume the need and the legitimacy of compulsion |
| Appeals | assume the audience is able to respond |
| Restatement trap | an option that repeats the statement is never an assumption |
| Strength | match the option to the statement exactly; reject wider or narrower |
| Direction | the belief belongs to the author, not the reader |

[summary]
Statement and Assumption questions ask what the author silently believes.
Find the hidden link, match the option at exactly the same strength,
and verify with the negation test. Reject restatements, out-of-scope
facts, and options that shift the belief from the author to the reader.
[/summary]
